Decision: **advisory_preparation**.

Research summary: `io.micronaut.security:micronaut-security-oauth2:5.3.2` is a pure client-side OAuth2/OIDC library (configuration classes, PKCE, state/nonce persistence, redirect handlers, token endpoint clients) driven entirely by application properties — no Docker image or live OAuth provider is needed, since tests can fake the authorization server with a local embedded Micronaut HTTP server. However, byte-level inspection of the jar shows its ID-token/JWKS validation classes (`DefaultReactiveOpenIdTokenResponseValidator`, `IdTokenLoginHandler`, `JwksUriSignatureFactory`) reference `io.micronaut.security.token.jwt.*` and `com.nimbusds.jwt.*` from the separate `io.micronaut.security:micronaut-security-jwt` artifact — checked both the POM and Gradle module metadata of `micronaut-security-oauth2`, `micronaut-security`, and `micronaut-reactor`, and none declare it as a dependency. Without adding it explicitly, the generated tests can't compile/exercise the OIDC token-validation path, a central piece of this library's functionality.

Returned JSON includes that one `testImplementation` dependency as deterministic setup, plus guidance (no deterministic entry) recommending a locally-faked discovery/JWKS server and self-signed test ID tokens rather than any external provider.
